  "wiki_only_topics": [
    {
      "topic": "Hesiod's account of Ate as daughter of Eris and her siblings",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Hesiod presented Ate as one of the several offspring of Eris, all of whom were personifications representing some of the many harms which can arise out of discord and strife. Hesiod particular associates Ate with her sister Dysnomia (Lawlessness). While listing the children of Eris, he lists both on the same line (230) of his Theogony and says they are \"much like one another\"."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Ate's role in Homer's Iliad, including the parable of Prayers and Book 19 speech",
      "quote_wikipedia": "In Homer, atē is something inflicted by the gods; it causes delusion, then folly, then disaster. Ate, as the personification of atē, receives its fullest development in Homer 's Iliad, his epic poem about the Trojan War. However, to what extent Homer may have considered Ate to be an actual divinity as opposed to a mere allegory is unclear."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Ate in Aeschylus's Oresteia and connection to Helen and Clytemnestra",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Ate also appears as an agent of Zeus' justice in Aeschylus 's tragic trilogy the Oresteia. In Agamemnon, the first play of the trilogy, Ate is linked with Helen of Troy, and Agamemnon's wife Clytemnestra, both of whom act as agents of Zeus' retribution."
    },
    {
      "topic": "The landing place of Ate in Phrygia and the founding of Troy",
      "quote_wikipedia": "According to the mythographer Apollodorus, when Ate was thrown down by Zeus, Ate landed in Phrygia at a place called \"the hill of the Phrygian Ate\", where the city of Troy was founded."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Ate as an agent of Zeus' divine retribution and justice",
      "quote_wikipedia": "Ate (or the impersonal atē) is often referred to as the agent (or instrument) of Zeus' divine retribution. In the Iliad, Zeus is begged to send Ate so that the denier of \"Prayers... may fall and pay full recompense.\""
    }
  ]
